{"seconds": 157.6, "exit": 0, "final": "So after 1. Nf7 Nxf2+ 2. Qxf2, Black still must deal with the knight on f7. If 2...Kxf7, then we're back to the line I analyzed above.\n\nSo it looks like Nf7 is indeed the key move, and it forces Black into a position where White wins by force (either the queen, the rook, or has a continuing mating attack).\n\nLet me verify the main line one more time:\n\n1. **Nf7** - sacrificing the knight to expose Black's king\n2. **Kxf7** (best try; other king moves or 2...Rxf7 also lose)\n3. **Qh5+** - checking the king and threatening the bishop\n4. **Kf6** (most fighting; 4...Kg8 allows 5. Qxg5+ winning the bishop, or 4...Ke7 allows 5. Qxe8+ winning the queen)\n5. **Qxg5+** - winning the bishop with check\n6. **Kf7** (or other moves)\n7. **Qxf5+** - forking king and pawn\n8. **Kg8** (or other moves) \n9. **Qxf8+** - winning the rook with check\n\nThis is a forced sequence where White sacrifices the knight to win Black's major pieces.\n\nSo the best move is **Nf7**.\n\nNow let me write the coaching note:\n\n\"Black's king lacks escape squares and the bishop on g5 is poorly placed. By sacrificing the knight with Nf7, you force the king to move into the open, where your queen can deliver checks while winning material (the bishop, rook, or queen). Always look for king hunts when your opponent's pieces are uncoordinated and the king has limited escape squares\u2014even sacrificing can be winning!\"\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nf7\", \"line\": [\"Nf7\", \"Kxf7\", \"Qh5+\", \"Kf6\", \"Qxg5+\", \"Kf7\"], \"motifs\": [\"sacrifice\", \"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3